I’ll first inspect the public pattern, metadata, and the framework’s analysis/tool guidance. Then I’ll identify candidate phases from the diffraction evidence, refine them cumulatively in GSAS-II, and independently replay the final project before writing the deliverables.

The chemistry is ZnO + WO₃ fired at 750 °C, so ZnWO₄ is the obvious product hypothesis, but I will not assume it is single-phase. I’m now extracting peak families and searching the permitted frozen structure library for Zn–W–O phases and chemically plausible residuals.

The peak positions already show a coherent sharp wurtzite-ZnO family (about 32.07, 34.74, 36.58, 47.85, 56.87, 63.16° after the common displacement), alongside much broader wolframite-like lines. I’m preparing physically screened ZnWO₄ analogues from multiple independent parents—including a doubled, ordered two-cation wolframite parent—so the missing exact-composition entry is not decided from a single convenient source.

The first two-phase refinement is decisively incomplete: it leaves an intense, internally consistent triplet at 23.45/23.90/24.71° plus companion lines at 26.91, 33.58, 34.48, and 41.57°. That family is diagnostic of residual low-symmetry WO₃. I’m adding WO₃ as a third phase and comparing the physically valid monoclinic/triclinic same-composition references rather than selecting one from the first match.

The three-phase inventory lowers the matched low-flexibility Rwp from about 20% to 11.8% immediately, with preliminary fractions near 72% ZnWO₄, 20% WO₃, and 8% ZnO. Those numbers are not final yet: the WO₃ source comparison is still running, and broadening/specimen/texture mechanisms have not been equilibrated.

The complete profile trial found real phase-dependent strain in ZnWO₄, but the fully simultaneous width model was not acceptable: common Lorentzian terms wanted negative values, and ZnO size became singular/bound-hitting. I’m restaging broadening into physically distinct branches (product strain versus size, WO₃ size versus strain, instrument-limited ZnO) and will retain only a branch that converges without frozen or drifting variables.

The three-phase inventory is now well supported: wolframite-type ZnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and wurtzite ZnO. I’m checking whether the remaining residuals demand texture/anisotropic broadening or atomic-coordinate changes, and I’ll carry the closest structural analogues through matched final refinements before choosing the submitted models.

The current difference curve points to intensity, not missing-line, errors—especially the three WO₃ reflections near 23–25°. Before touching coordinates, I’m testing whether the modestly better correlated size/strain model reproduces across independent starts, then screening smooth background, polarization, and surface-roughness branches on equal footing.

The hybrid broadening model is reproducible from small and medium starts: phase fractions agree to ~0.001 wt% absolute, cells to about 4×10⁻⁶ Å, and the calculated curve is unchanged. One deliberately distant start is taking a damped route; I’m letting that finish so the correlation is judged by predictive stability rather than by its coefficient correlation alone.

The two completed starts support retaining the correlated ZnWO₄ size/strain subspace because it is predictive: both converge to ~0.284 μm apparent size and ~6060 ppm strain with indistinguishable fractions. The background/polarization/roughness comparison is running now; any branch that reaches a bound or gives nonphysical coefficients will be discarded even if its Rwp is lower.

Polarization is clearly supported and remains physical (about 0.52); surface roughness is not—its branches collapse to bounds and are being excluded. A 12-term smooth Chebyshev background gives a small stable gain without materially changing fractions. I’m now screening March–Dollase directions phase by phase; this is the key test for whether the WO₃ low-angle intensity mismatch is geometric rather than structural.

A strong, coherent mechanism has emerged: WO₃ March–Dollase texture along its [001] axis cuts Rwp from 9.06% to 4.03% with a moderate ratio of 1.291 and no frozen variables. Other WO₃ axes are much worse, and ZnWO₄/ZnO texture effects are negligible. I’ll verify that direction from opposing starts and then retest broadening and source analogues with this texture present—the large gain makes those earlier no-texture rankings provisional.

The [001] WO₃ texture correction specifically fixes the 23.44° (002), 23.89° (020), and 24.69° (20̅2) intensity family rather than merely lowering a global metric. The uniaxial-size screen is still running across all three phases; after it completes I’ll repeat only supported candidates on top of the textured model.

The no-texture uniaxial-size screen confirms that WO₃ anisotropic size can mimic the same intensity family only by driving an equatorial size to an effective limit/frozen state, so it is not a valid substitute for texture. A mild ZnWO₄ [101] size anisotropy remains plausible and will be retested after texture. The corresponding strain screen is underway.

The strain sweep is slower because several deliberately over-flexible branches require damping. I’m retaining only converged, positive solutions with no frozen terms; the textured WO₃ model itself remains clean, so these stress tests cannot displace it merely by producing a lower transient Rwp.

The full post-texture cross-check is now running: opposing texture starts, WO₃ size versus strain anisotropy, ZnWO₄ [101] size/strain, background orders 8–16, and retries of the previously rejected X/Y and roughness terms. This should leave a single mechanism-complete nuisance model before any coordinate refinement.

The post-texture model is stable from ratios 0.5 and 1.5, both returning 1.2911 and identical fractions. Two mild broadening terms survive cleanly—ZnWO₄ [101] size anisotropy and WO₃ [001] strain anisotropy—while roughness and X still fail bounds. A positive Y term and higher smooth-background order now merit a final combined restart test before analogue swapping.

The first combined restart revealed that Y is not robust once the supported anisotropic broadening and higher-order background are present—it tries to cross its physical lower bound from one start. I’m completing the other starts and will drop Y unless all valid restarts converge to the same positive value and curve.

The conservative mechanism-complete template is now defined: 16-term smooth background, physical polarization, shift-only position correction, reproducible WO₃ [001] texture, mild ZnWO₄ [101] size anisotropy, and mild WO₃ [001] strain anisotropy; Y, X, and roughness are excluded. I’m swapping the structural parents into this same template now, with cells adapted before refinement and coordinates still fixed.

The matched swaps are refining now. The comparison includes four wolframite P2/c parents, the triclinic Zn/Cu parent, the doubled ordered parent, and scheelite, plus two alternative low-symmetry WO₃ parents. Each starts from an adapted cell, settles scales/cells at lower flexibility, then restores the same texture and broadening terms.

So far the comparison is behaving as intended: no coordinates are moving, and failed/bound profile substitutions are being logged rather than treated as evidence against a parent. I’m waiting for the lower-symmetry and ordered-cell restarts, which are the slowest because their adapted cells need more damping.

The analogue run is still active but making progress; these are full re-equilibrations rather than stick-pattern scores. Once complete, I’ll inspect any bound-hitting swap individually and restage it from a simpler profile before deciding whether it is genuinely inferior or merely inconclusive.

The clean matched swaps identify two serious ZnWO₄ parents: Zn/Cd-derived P2/c currently leads at Rwp 3.871%, with Zn/Cu-derived P2/c at 3.934%; both remain credible. Monoclinic WO₃ leads, while the triclinic WO₃ parent is close at 3.977% and remains in play. The P1, doubled ordered, and scheelite swaps hit profile bounds, so I’m restaging those with isotropic broadening before rejecting them.

The bounded topology swaps are now being judged under a simpler isotropic profile. This matters because a wolframite-specific [101] broadening axis is not a fair requirement for scheelite or a doubled ordered framework; only a clean simplified restart can make their rejection meaningful.

The simplified restarts are decisive and clean: triclinic product, doubled ordered, and scheelite remain at Rwp ≈29–33% with no frozen variables, so their peak topologies are incompatible. I’m now repeating the WO₃ finalists cumulatively with the leading Zn/Cd-derived wolframite parent and screening equivalent texture axes in each WO₃ setting.

The cumulative comparison is running with the leading product parent fixed in the inventory. For each WO₃ source I’m allowing one March–Dollase term but changing its crystallographic axis discretely; this avoids unfairly penalizing a source whose cell setting maps the observed plate texture to a different index direction.

Several direct texture-axis swaps were numerically inconclusive because changing the axis while retaining the old anisotropic strain made the initial objective invalid. I’ve restaged them correctly: texture is solved first with WO₃ strain fixed, then a fresh positive uniaxial strain is introduced in the candidate setting.

The cumulative ranking is stable: Zn/Cd-derived wolframite + monoclinic WO₃ gives Rwp 3.871%; replacing WO₃ with the clean triclinic solution gives 3.915%, so it remains a credible runner-up. Alternative-axis restarts that could not evaluate are treated as inconclusive, not as rejections. I’m doing the final clean position check, then beginning element/site coordinate blocks on these finalists.

The final position test confirms specimen displacement is the stable branch: shift-only gives Rwp 3.871% with reproducible cells. A joint zero/shift start can shave 0.0003% from Rwp but another plausible start drifts to different correction/cell values, so that correlated branch is rejected. Atomic coordinates are now the only new degrees of freedom being tested.

Coordinate trials are being run one block at a time—Zn/W/O sites in ZnWO₄, each W/O site and element-wide blocks in WO₃, and the ZnO oxygen coordinate. I’ll retain only improvements that survive recombination and produce sensible metal–oxygen distances; a large Rwp gain alone is not enough.

All useful single-block coordinate moves pass basic geometry screening. The strongest effect is the ZnWO₄ cation y coordinates (Rwp 2.916%, W-only 2.956%); the refined Zn–O and W–O shells remain chemically sensible. I’m recombining oxygen and WO₃ blocks and repeating the same blocks for the near-tied ZnWO₄ and WO₃ parents so source choice is not locked in by starting coordinates.

The recombination run is comparing equal coordinate flexibility across three finalists: Zn/Cd-derived wolframite + monoclinic WO₃, Zn/Cu-derived wolframite + monoclinic WO₃, and Zn/Cd-derived wolframite + triclinic WO₃. This will show whether the earlier source advantage survives once the same atomic blocks are allowed to relax.

The combined coordinate models are still converging cleanly; no frozen terms have appeared in the completed branches so far. I’m letting the full oxygen blocks finish because they are the critical overfitting/geometry check, even though the smaller cation blocks already account for most of the intensity improvement.

Equal-flexibility atomic refinement collapses the Zn/Cd- and Zn/Cu-derived wolframite parents to effectively the same solution (Rwp 2.70836% vs 2.70816%); that tiny source-dependent difference is not meaningful. Monoclinic WO₃ still beats triclinic by ~0.061% after matched blocks. I’m checking geometry and repeating the full coordinate improvement from deliberately different nuisance starts before deciding how many oxygen coordinates to retain.

The final all-coordinate structures are physically sound: ZnWO₄ has Zn–O 1.99–2.24 Å and W–O 1.77–2.18 Å; monoclinic WO₃ retains reasonable distorted WO₆ shells. The two product parents converge to coordinate differences below ~1×10⁻⁴ fraction. The independent nuisance-start replay is now testing whether that same structure and fraction recur.

The coordinate gain is reproducible for the two valid nuisance starts: background orders 16 and 18 converge to nearly identical ZnWO₄ coordinates, WO₃ texture (~1.295), and fractions (~71.52/21.29/7.19 wt%). A deliberately poor low-order/broadening start drives WO₃ size to an implausible 0.001 μm and is rejected. I’m doing one final displacement-parameter screen now; negative or scale-dominated Uiso branches will be discarded.

The displacement screen is informative: WO₃ oxygen Uiso values become negative and are rejected; ZnO Uiso adds essentially nothing. Heavy-atom Uiso terms are physical and improve the fit, but they also correlate with phase scales. I’m testing only the defensible W-site terms from several starts and background orders; fractions must reproduce before those terms can enter the final model.

The defensible heavy-W displacement model is stable from Uiso starts 0.003–0.020 Å² and across background orders: it converges to ZnWO₄ W ≈0.0114 Å² and WO₃ W ≈0.004–0.006 Å², with fractions stable within 0.02 wt%. I’m now simplifying profile terms again after atomic refinement; the ZnWO₄ size anisotropy has nearly collapsed to isotropic and will be removed if its deletion is neutral.

The post-atomic deletion/simplification sweep is running now. It explicitly removes texture, polarization, each heavy-W Uiso block, product size/strain, and WO₃ strain, and compares background orders 14/16/18/20. This is the last model-selection pass before phase deletion tests and export.

The sweep is taking longer on deletion branches because removing a genuine mechanism forces common width and scale terms to re-equilibrate. I’m keeping those branches running to convergence so a higher Rwp reflects predictive failure, not an unfinished restart.

Post-atomic simplification is complete. ZnWO₄ size and WO₃ strain anisotropy are no longer supported; replacing both with isotropic terms costs only 0.00003% Rwp while removing two parameters. Texture, polarization, both phase-size/strain families, and heavy-W Uiso all fail deletion decisively. A 16-term background is retained because higher orders lower χ² slightly but are disfavored by BIC and add curvature.

The first phase-deletion implementation exposed a GSAS-II replay issue: disabling a linked phase left cached curves after a failed objective evaluation for two branches. I’m rerunning deletions by fixing the deleted scale to zero and freezing atomic variables, while still re-equilibrating backgrounds, cells, scales, texture, and profile terms. The earlier deletion numbers for WO₃/ZnO are therefore not being used.

The final three-phase inventory passes clean deletion tests: removing ZnWO₄, WO₃, or ZnO raises Rwp from 2.666% to 34.53%, 19.85%, or 6.29%, respectively, and restores the expected multi-line families. I’m now producing the submission files from the simplified, physically screened project and will independently replay that exact GPX before handing it over.

One finalist audit found a bookkeeping mismatch: the triclinic WO₃ parent has four W and twelve O sites, so an earlier “all-coordinate” branch addressed only the monoclinic labels and was not a fair completed swap. I’m correcting that now with element-driven triclinic blocks; its much larger parameter count will also be judged against BIC and geometry, not raw Rwp alone.

The corrected triclinic test is refining 4 W + 12 O sites versus 2 W + 6 O sites in monoclinic WO₃. Even if the lower-symmetry model wins slightly in Rwp, it must deliver a coherent physical structure and enough χ² reduction to justify roughly two dozen extra coordinate variables.

The corrected triclinic finalist is now complete. After all 4 W + 12 O coordinates, heavy-W Uiso, and the same simplified profile, it gives Rwp 2.676% with 104 variables; monoclinic gives 2.666% with 76 variables and lower χ². Monoclinic WO₃ is therefore selected on both fit and parsimony. I’m generating the final GPX/CIF/profile/JSON/report set now.

The final refinement/export completed successfully. I’m now doing the independent replay and cross-checking the project, CIFs, fractions, and profile row-for-row before handing it over.

One export detail needed correction: GSAS-II included the nearest point just above the nominal 100° limit in its 8,284-point refinement, while the first CSV export omitted that single calculated point. I’m aligning `fit.csv` exactly to the points actually used by the saved project, then replaying that corrected artifact set.

The replay converged to the same solution: fractions changed by at most 0.000055 percentage point and Rwp by 0.000010 percentage point. Its largest pointwise curve change was about one count—well below counting noise—so I’m recording stability on that physically meaningful scale and continuing with the independent CIF geometry screen.

The independent checks now pass: all CIFs reproduce the intended formulas and space groups with full occupancies and sensible coordination, `fit.csv` matches all 8,284 refined observations, and a fresh two-pass GSAS-II replay reproduces the fractions and fit within numerical noise.

The crystalline inventory is:

| Phase | Structure | Weight fraction |
|---|---|---:|
| ZnWO₄ | Wolframite, P2/c | 0.72143 (72.143 wt%) |
| WO₃ | Monoclinic, P2₁/c | 0.20692 (20.692 wt%) |
| ZnO | Wurtzite, P6₃mc | 0.07165 (7.165 wt%) |

Final fit: Rwp 2.6664%, Rp 2.0274%, GOF 0.9951. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases. Amorphous content cannot be quantified; practical fraction uncertainty is approximately ±0.2–0.4 percentage point.

